<article class="env-lemma" id="0B40">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0B40">Lemma <span data-tag="0B40">33.44.12</span>.</a> Let $k$ be a field. Let $X$ be a proper curve over $k$. Let $\mathcal{L}$ be an invertible $\mathcal{O}_ X$-module. </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
If $\mathcal{L}$ has a nonzero section, then $\deg (\mathcal{L}) \geq 0$. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
If $\mathcal{L}$ has a nonzero section $s$ which vanishes at a point, then $\deg (\mathcal{L}) > 0$. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
If $\mathcal{L}$ and $\mathcal{L}^{-1}$ have nonzero sections, then $\mathcal{L} \cong \mathcal{O}_ X$. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
If $\deg (\mathcal{L}) \leq 0$ and $\mathcal{L}$ has a nonzero section, then $\mathcal{L} \cong \mathcal{O}_ X$. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
If $\mathcal{N} \to \mathcal{L}$ is a nonzero map of invertible $\mathcal{O}_ X$-modules, then $\deg (\mathcal{L}) \geq \deg (\mathcal{N})$ and if equality holds then it is an isomorphism. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Let $s$ be a nonzero section of $\mathcal{L}$. Since $X$ is a curve, we see that $s$ is a regular section. Hence there is an effective Cartier divisor $D \subset X$ and an isomorphism $\mathcal{L} \to \mathcal{O}_ X(D)$ mapping $s$ the canonical section $1$ of $\mathcal{O}_ X(D)$, see Divisors, Lemma <a href="/tag/01X0" data-tag="01X0">31.15.10</a>. Then $\deg (\mathcal{L}) = \deg (D)$ by Lemma <a href="/tag/0AYY" data-tag="0AYY">33.44.9</a>. As $\deg (D) \geq 0$ and $= 0$ if and only if $D = \emptyset $, this proves (1) and (2). In case (3) we see that $\deg (\mathcal{L}) = 0$ and $D = \emptyset $. Similarly for (4). To see (5) apply (1) and (4) to the invertible sheaf 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  \mathcal{L} \otimes _{\mathcal{O}_ X} \mathcal{N}^{\otimes -1} = \mathop{\mathcal{H}\! \mathit{om}}\nolimits _{\mathcal{O}_ X}(\mathcal{N}, \mathcal{L})  \]
</div>
    <p>
       which has degree $\deg (\mathcal{L}) - \deg (\mathcal{N})$ by Lemma <a href="/tag/0AYX" data-tag="0AYX">33.44.7</a>. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>